RECORD OF PROCEEDINGS FOR THE GOVERNING BODY
OF THE CITY OF CHEYENNE
Office of City Clerk
February 28, 2011
The Governing Body of the City of Cheyenne met in regular session on this date
beginning at 6:00 p.m. in Council Chambers.  Present were: MAYOR - Richard L. Kaysen;
COUNCIL MEMBERS - Mr. Allen, Ms. Ash, Mr. Brown, Ms. Broyles, Mr. Collins, Mr.
Pierson, Dr. Rinne, Mr. Spiker and Mr. Valdez.  Also present: Carol Intlekofer, City Clerk; Barb
Dorr, City Treasurer, and Dan White, City Attorney.  The pledge of allegiance was recited.
Consent Agenda. (All agenda items listed with the designation of [CA] are considered to
be routine items by the governing body and will be enacted by one motion.  There will be no
separate discussion on these items unless a member of the governing body so requests and
support by two other members is received.  Any item removed from the Consent Agenda will be
considered in its normal sequence on the agenda.)  Upon request by Ms. Ash, and pursuant to
support received by Mr. Collins and Dr. Rinne, agenda items #16, and #23(b) and (c) were
removed from the Consent Agenda. Mr. Collins moved to approve remaining Consent Agenda
items, seconded by Mr. Brown.  Motion carried. Voting “yes” – all members of the governing
body.
[CA]
Minutes from Regular Meeting of the Governing Body on February 14, 2011.  Motion
(per Consent Agenda) carried.
Mr. Allen moved to approve payment of vouchers as presented, seconded by Mr. Valdez.
Mr. Brown declared a conflict of interest (business interest) due to a voucher payable to Ameri-
Tech HVAC Services, and vacated Council Chambers.  Inquiry was made concerning the
following vouchers: Accurate Construction and Electrical Services ($5,585.00), replace fixtures
with energy efficient light fixtures at Transfer Station; Creative Concrete, Inc. ($8,255.00), off-
site improvements utilizing escrow account funds; Labor Ready ($4,733.37), temporary staffing
at the Transfer Station; Maxwell Photography ($496.00), portraits to update Police Dept. picture
board; Health Reach ($300), deposition expense involving a workers compensation claim, and
Aavon Moving and Storage ($175.00), rent for 2 storage container units at Depot Plaza.
Providing information on the aforementioned vouchers for their respective areas/projects were
Jim Elias, Public Works Director; Doug Vetter, City Engineer; Brian Kozak, Chief of Police;
Dan White, City Attorney, and Carol Intlekofer, City Clerk.  Upon query, Mr. Elias also
provided information on staffing levels for the Sanitation Division and possible positions to be
filled in the near future. Motion carried. Voting “yes” – all members of the governing body
present in Council Chambers.  Following announcement of the vote, Mr. Brown returned to
Council Chambers.
Mayor Kaysen opened a Public Hearing to receive public comments and input for
renewal of existing City liquor licenses (retail, restaurant, limited retail, resort, bar and grill
licenses, microbrewery and winery permits) filed with the City Clerk’s Office.   City Clerk Carol
Intlekofer noted for the record that the following current liquor licensees did not file for renewal
```

### Page 2 of 11

```text
or were not eligible to submit a renewal application for the 2011-2012 licensing term: Wyo-Mex
II, Inc. d/b/a On the Border (bar & grill liquor license); Mantiff Cheyenne Hospitality, LLC d/b/a
Ramada Hitching Post Inn (retail liquor license); Primos Cheyenne d/b/a Scotty’s Pizza Factory
(restaurant liquor license), and Jose Luis Gomez-Salcedo d/b/a Los 3 Caballos (restaurant liquor
license).  Upon inquiry, Officer Steve Neisen, Police Dept., provided information on compliance
checks conducted for licensees’ TIPS (Training for Intervention ProcedureS) training records,
and Police Chief Brian Kozak reviewed  underage compliance checks recently conducted, and
stated he would be visiting with one licensee who had failed two compliance checks.  He advised
the department hopes to conduct compliance checks throughout the year as well as offering an
on-line electronic option for licensees to keep up with employee TIPS training requirements.
Upon inquiry, City Attorney Dan White briefly reviewed the status of the Hitching Post retail
liquor license relative to the current licensee’s bankruptcy status, and explained that the current
licensee/Chapter 7 trustee had responsibility to file for renewal, of which they did not do. He
explained instead the licensee attempted to transfer the license, which did not move forward, and
that the renewal application filing deadline had now passed. Upon further inquiry regarding TIPS
training, Chief Kozak and Officer Neisen advised an interpretation from the Court clarified new
employees have 90 days in which to receive TIPS training. Officer Neisen reviewed ordinance
language and suggested a review of current city code wording for possible clarification.  There
being no further comments and purpose of the hearing having been met, Mayor Kaysen closed
the hearing and referred the applications to Finance Committee.
“An Ordinance amending Section 9.16.100 (B),  Open House Assembly, of Chapter 9.16,
Offenses By or Against Minors, of Title 9, Public Peace and Welfare, of the Code of the City of
Cheyenne, Wyoming, pertaining to the definition of ‘Adult’ and providing a penalty for
violations.” Reporting for Public Services Committee, Mr. Collins moved to approve on third
and final reading, seconded by Ms. Broyles. Discussion included the following topics: Whether
the ordinance will make a difference as a deterrent to curb underage drinking/hosting of parties;
penalty clause; current fines assessed by municipal court judges and offenses requiring a “must
appear” in court; concerns with individuals who are 21 years of age or older providing alcohol to
family members or friends who are minors, and strengthening the penalty for minor in
possession (MIP) offenses and contributing to the delinquency of a minor.  Upon inquiry
regarding citations issued pursuant to a recent underage party involving alcohol, Police Chief
Brian Kozak advised the 18 year old hosting the party was cited with a minor in possession
violation and the parent was cited for contributing to the delinquency of a minor.  Chief Kozak
clarified the municipal judge has the authority to determine what offenses require a court
appearance.  Motion carried.  Voting “yes” – Mr. Allen, Ms. Broyles, Mayor Kaysen, Dr. Rinne,
Mr. Spiker and Mr. Valdez.  Voting “no” – Ms. Ash, Mr. Brown, Mr. Collins and Mr. Pierson.
(#3913)
“An Ordinance amending Section 5.12.060, Sales and Consumption--Restrictions, of
Chapter 5.12, Alcoholic Beverages, of Title 5, Business Licenses and Regulations, of the Code
of the City of Cheyenne, Wyoming, to authorize the issuance of a Malt Beverage Permit or
Catering Permit limited to malt beverages, with open container waiver, in conjunction with adult
softball/baseball games and tournaments at the Pioneer Park Baseball Complex.”  Major Dorr,
speaking for himself and also on behalf of members of First Baptist Church, expressed support
for the Cheyenne Grizzlies baseball team and activities but spoke in opposition to the ordinance.
```

### Page 3 of 11

```text
He cited concerns regarding beer sales as a revenue source for the team; alcohol consumption
and sales in the proximity of youth; ball games as a family oriented event; TIPS compliance;
liability and responsibility; potential negative influence on youth attending the games, and
repercussions of serving malt beverages at ball games.  He offered ideas on other ways for the
Grizzlies to raise funds.  Ron Kailey, Cheyenne Grizzlies owner, spoke in favor of the ordinance
and stated many fans have requested that malt beverages be served at the ball games, that all
servers will be TIPS trained, and that he would like the opportunity to provide sales.  Reporting
for Finance Committee, Mr. Valdez moved to approve on second reading, seconded by Ms. Ash.
Mr. Valdez moved to amend Section 5.12.060, Subsection10(c) by removing the word “will”
and replacing it with the word “shall”, seconded by Mr. Pierson.  Motion carried.  Voting “yes”
– all members of the governing body with the exception of Mr. Valdez voting “no”.  Mr. Valdez
moved to amend to add a new paragraph (d) in Subsection 10 to read as follows: “The
dispensing and consumption boundary shall be signed “No one under the age of 21 years
allowed,” and “No alcoholic beverages allowed outside the dispensing and consumption
boundary,” seconded by Mr. Pierson.  John Palmer questioned wording of the proposed
amendment relative to whether the location of the consumption area would be in a good location
in which to view the game.  Discussion included the following topics:  City staff involved in
approving dispensing and consumption areas; consumption vs. non-consumption areas; keeping
family members together; local fans vs. those in attendance supporting an opposing ball team
being seated in the same consumption area, and City’s current procedures in reviewing and
determining approval for sales and consumption areas. Upon inquiry, John Knepper, Staff
Attorney, clarified the amendment provides for the dispensing and consumption area(s) to be
accessed only by those individuals 21 years of age or older.  Further discussion included:
Purpose of the amendment (separation of minors from alcohol dispensing and consumption area,
and notification that beverages must stay within the designated boundaries); alcohol
consumption separation area from minors; Youth Alternatives facility located across the street;
consistency in application of requirements relative to other City-owned property where alcohol
and youth are present at community events; concerns with alcohol presence for family oriented
events on City property; responsible drinking practices; separating family members attending
games; commitment by the Grizzlies owner to monitor the area and provide security, and state-
wide “Where Do You Draw The Line” campaign.  Motion to amend failed.  Voting “yes” -- Mr.
Collins, Mr. Pierson and Mr. Spiker. Voting “no” -- Mr. Allen, Ms. Ash, Mr. Brown, Ms.
Broyles, Dr. Rinne, Mr. Valdez, and Mayor Kaysen. Upon inquiry, Rick Parish, Parks and
Recreation Director, provided information on special events upon Parks and Recreation City-
owned properties and review of proposed designated dispensing and consumption areas by
various City staff.  Further comments followed on other parks and areas within the City where
malt beverage sales and consumption is allowed.  Upon inquiry, City Clerk Carol Intlekofer
explained the current malt beverage permit application and staff review process.  Discussion
continued on dispensing/consumption areas; consistency in applying requirements; basic intent
of the ordinance; proposed ordinance wording that adds games not just softball tournaments as is
currently applicable to Converse and Brimmer ball parks; number of permits an applicant can
apply for within a calendar year, and consumption permits vs. malt beverage permits for sales
involving consumption areas. Upon inquiry, Mr. Knepper and Ms. Intlekofer provided
information on Section 5.12.060, Subsection C. 2. of the ordinance affiliated with consumption
permits (no sales) vs. malt beverage permits (sales).  Mr. Kailey confirmed  Cheyenne Grizzlies
is a for-profit organization, clarified fans are requesting malt beverages at games and stated
```

