“An Ordinance amending  Ordinance  No. 3419,  which  established a new compensation
schedule for members of the City Council of the City of Cheyenne, to clarify the manner in
which compensation is to be computed, and providing for an effective date.”  Mr. Valdez moved
to approve on second reading, seconded by Mr. Brown. Motion carried.  Voting “yes” – all
members of the governing body.
“An Ordinance repealing in its entirety,  Chapter 8.44,  Solid Waste, and creating a new
Chapter 8.44, Solid Waste, of Title 8, Health and Safety, of the Code of the City of Cheyenne,
Wyoming.”  Rick Fierro, President, RAM Trucking; Drew Kelley, Wyoming Disposal Systems,
and Jim Clark, Countryside Disposal, each spoke in opposition to the ordinance with regard to
private businesses providing roll-off container services.  Topics and concerns presented
included: Difficulty private businesses have in competing with the City in the area of providing
roll-off container services; proposed changes contained in the ordinance that will affect their
businesses and employees; construction and demolition waste, and recyclable disposal services
by the private section; investment in the community by private businesses; City roll-off fee
structure, and current restrictions private haulers have in offering roll-off container services
within the City.  Frank Cole, developer, discussed automated curbside pickup of solid waste and
recyclables and his concerns regarding unsightly container storage placement on non-pickup
days; proposed schedule of pickups included in the ordinance, and support for free enterprise
relative to private haulers.  John Palmer questioned what new changes were being proposed in
the ordinance vs. current City Code.  Mr. Valdez moved to approve on second reading, seconded
by Mr. Brown.  Mr. Brown moved to amend by substitute ordinance, seconded by Mr. Valdez.
Upon inquiry, Jim Elias, Public Works Director, advised the substitute addresses the allowable
practice of bringing recyclables to the City blue bins by amending Section 8.44.010, Subsection
F. to add two sentences at the end of the paragraph as follows: “Waste generated outside the city
may not be brought into the city for disposal.  Waste generated outside the city is prohibited
from being placed into alleys, vacant lots, residential or commercial containers within the City
(including those located in parks or other city facilities).  County residents may use private
haulers or dispose of waste at the transfer station. THIS PARAGRAPH DOES NOT PROHIBIT
COLLECTORS OF RECYCLABLE MATERIAL GENERATED OR COLLECTED OUTSIDE
THE CITY FROM BRINGING SUCH MATERIAL INTO THE CITY AND PLACING IT
INTO THE BLUE BIN CONTAINERS OR DROPPING IT AT THE TRANSFER STATION;
SUBJECT TO THE FEES, LIMITATIONS AND REQUIREMENTS OF THIS CHAPTER.
RECYCLABLE MATERIAL GENERATED OR COLLECTED OUTSIDE THE CITY MAY
NOT BE PLACED INTO RESIDENTIAL OR COMMERCIAL RECYCLE CONTAINERS
```

### Page 5 of 11

```text
INSIDE THE CITY NOR MAY THEY BE PLACED OUTSIDE BLUE BIN CONTAINERS IF
THOSE CONTAINERS ARE FULL.”  Pursuant to comment on lack of a penalty affiliated with
disposing of items on the ground if blue bins are full, Mr. Elias advised their goal is to encourage
recycling from residents outside the city limits, and noted further review of littering penalties
will be conducted prior to final reading of the ordinance.  Upon inquiry, he discussed wording of
“containers” in Section 8.44.030, Subsection E.6.; provided information on the City’s
responsibility for the collection, transfer and disposal of all waste within the City; private vs.
City roll-off container services, which has not been specifically codified in the past; collection
programs utilized in other communities, and explained that the City’s roll-off container
operations are not an expansion (i.e. increasing the number of containers) of current services.
Mr. Elias stated staff is still working on the comprehensive draft of the Solid Waste Master Plan,
which involves reviewing and analyzing several areas, including financial and operational
aspects as well as the Integrated Solid Waste Plan.  Discussion continued on the cost to process
waste; whether the issue involving private businesses to provide roll-off containers was
instrumental to be included in the proposed ordinance at this time; allowing private roll-off
providers to participate in discussions concerning proposed ordinance changes; ordinance
wording relative to billing (errors) adjustments and time frame in which customers must provide
notification of same, and City’s cost to dispose of waste and maintain City landfill.  During
discussion, Mr. Elias provided further information on the topics raised.  Dr. Rinne raised a point
of order concerning the allowance of further public comment. Mayor Kaysen ruled he would
deviate slightly from the normal public participation process and would allow additional
comments from the public to be made following a 10 minute recess. Mayor Kaysen reconvened
the meeting at 8:35 p.m.  The following individuals spoke further on the proposed ordinance:
Mr. Cole; Mr. Fierro; Brian Clark, Countryside Disposal, and Mr. Kelley.  Comments included
concerns with customers responsibility to notice and request an adjustment for any discrepancies
in billing statements with a 60 day time limit; blue bins usage volume on weekends; clarification
needed for ordinance wording regarding roll-off containers, and respect for services provided by
private businesses. Motion to amend by substitute carried.  Voting “yes” -- all members of the
governing body with the exception of Ms. Ash and Ms. Broyles voting “no”.  Discussion
continued on ordinance Section 8.44.050, Subsection B. 1. involving residential pickup services
and proposed ordinance wording indicating residential collection services would be suspended
during weeks that begin with a Monday of a 5th week; operational efficiency, and delivery of
services overall in conjunction with assessment of fees.  Mr. Elias clarified that the language
contained in Section 8.44.050, Subsection B.1. indicates the City may suspend services during
that time frame, with proper notice, to provide for staff training or equipment maintenance.
Main motion as amended by substitute carried.  Voting “yes” -- all members of the governing
body with the exception of Ms. Ash, Ms. Broyles and Mr. Collins voting “no”.
