th
Announcement was made of a Public Hearing to be held March 14, 2011 at 6:00 p.m. in
City Council Chambers, 2101 O'Neil Avenue, Cheyenne, Wyoming, to hear public comment
regarding the intent of the City of Cheyenne to submit an application for funding for the
Wyoming Business Council's Business Ready Community Grant and Loan, Managed Data
Center Cost Reduction Program, to assist in the establishment of a Data Center, funding
electricity and broadband expenses for three years.
Under other City business:
John Palmer expressed his concerns with the public participation format utilized at City
Council meetings, requesting Council members consider changing the process, and the ability to
find agenda information on the City’s website.  Comment was made later under other business
that meeting agenda information is also available through staff at the Municipal Building.
Don Threewitt, State Historic Preservation Board, provided a brief update on the Board’s
activities and projects, and invited members of the governing body to a Board Strategic Planning
session to be held on March 12, 2011.  Mayor Kaysen requested Mr. Threewitt submit  details on
the meeting place and time that would be shared with City Council members.
Upon inquiry regarding a letter Council members received from Safehouse, Dan White,
City Attorney, provided information regarding the lease of City-owned land to Attention Homes
and discussions held between Attention Homes and Safehouse relative to assignment of the
property lease to Safehouse.  He stated his concerns with assignment of the lease without City
approval, noting assignment requirements contained in the existing lease, terms of the lease
regarding ownership of buildings that have been placed upon the City’s property, and services
that are to be provided (i.e. youth crisis services) at the site.  He indicated communication was
made with Safehouse representatives encouraging review of lease provisions with the City, and
discussed research conducted regarding ownership of permanent fixtures that have been placed
on the property. He noted one building on the site was constructed with grant funds for the
specific purpose of providing youth crisis services by Attention Homes.  He further stated that he
had presented a proposed lease to Safehouse for consideration to submit to the City Council for
approval but that Safehouse “did not agree” and the assignment of the property lease from
Attention Homes to Safehouse has occurred.  Upon inquiry, he reviewed possible options on
how to potentially proceed regarding assignment of the lease.
There being no further other business, City Attorney Dan White requested the governing
body meet in executive session for the sole purpose of discussing a potential lawsuit against
Laramie County to challenge the validity of recently approved County subdivision regulations.
Mr. Pierson moved to meet in executive session, seconded by Mr. Collins.  Motion carried.
Voting “yes” -- all members of the governing body.  Mayor Kaysen announced members would
meet in executive session in Council Chambers, not open to the public; requested the City
Attorney, City Clerk, and other persons approved by the City Attorney, be present at the
executive session, and stated there would be no action taken by the governing body as a result of
